Discussion
Traditionally, feature selection methods can be categorized into three different types: the filter, wrapper, and
embedded methods35. The strategy for the filter method was based on ranking the performance or mutual information of each feature, which was widely used in many domains because of their simplicity. The wrapper method worked as a black box by selecting features based on the performance of the classifier, which often showed great computational complexity and only worked well in limited classifiers. The embedded method was similar to the wrapper method; however, it focuses on reducing the amount of computational time required. All of the methods above aimed to select the top-ranked features purely based on statistical or predictive performance and disregarded the biological meaning of the gene features. Therefore, the selected gene features often lack biological insights and cannot be applied to further experimental validation.
